{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If you walk in My statutes, etc.</b> Rashi explained: that you toil in Torah, etc. Even though Rashi demonstrated this from the superfluity of the verses — for behold, the fulfillment of the commandments is already stated, etc. — nevertheless, also from the standpoint of language the matter requires explanation. And it is, that his intent is to make three divisions in this verse, corresponding to which he said three times ״וְנָתַתִּי״ ('and I will give'). In the first division he said ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ״, concerning the toil in Torah and the fixing of set times for Torah — a חֹק ('statute') that shall not pass. And concerning this he used the expression ״בְּחֻקֹּתַי״, for his intent is not regarding the statutory commandments (חֻקִּים), for we do not find regarding them anywhere in the entire Torah the language of הֲלִיכָה ('walking'), for nowhere in the entire Torah is it said ״לְכוּ בְּחֻקֹּתַי״ — hear from this that this הֲלִיכָה speaks of a matter that HaKadosh Baruch Hu does not command, save that the person does so on his own; and perforce you must explain it regarding a matter that the person produces through habit, for through habituation the matter becomes a second nature. And this fits only regarding one who fixes statutes and set times for Torah, and he trains himself to come to the study hall at a known time, until the matter becomes nature for his legs. And this accords with the opinion of the Midrash (Vayikra Rabbah 35:1), which says: ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ״ — this is what is written (Psalms 119:59): ״חִשַּׁבְתִּי דְּרָכַי וָאָשִׁיבָה רַגְלַי אֶל עֵדֹתֶיךָ״. David said before HaKadosh Baruch Hu: Master of the Universe, every single day I would plan to go to a certain place, and my legs would bring me to the study halls, etc. And this is only because he would fix set times for Torah — a חֹק that shall not pass — until רַגְלוֹהִי אִנּוּן עָרְבִין ('his legs are borrowed' [i.e., accustomed]) to carry him to the study halls without intention, for they were already habituated to this. And therefore there was no place to command this to a person, for regarding a matter done without intention no commandment falls upon it — for there is no commandment for the legs. Rather, it speaks of the 'if' — if it so happened that he fixed set times for Torah until at that time his legs go on their own at that time which is called a חֹק; and concerning this he said ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ״. And corresponding to this he said ״וְנָתַתִּי גִּשְׁמֵיכֶם בְּעִתָּם״, for corresponding to one who fixes set times for Torah he said that his reward is that the rains shall come בְּעִתָּם ('in their time'); as our Sages of blessed memory said (Ta'anit 7a): the rains are withheld only on account of the sin of neglecting Torah, as it says (Ecclesiastes 10:18): ״בַּעֲצַלְתַּיִם יִמַּךְ הַמְּקָרֶה״." "And perhaps it is for this reason that at the giving of the Torah it was said: ״אַף שָׁמַיִם נָטְפוּ מִפְּנֵי אֱלֹהִים זֶה סִינַי״ (Psalms 68:9) — to teach that in the merit of the study of Torah, which is from the heavens, the rain would descend from the heavens; for if there is no Torah there is no grain (Avot 3:21), and in the merit of the חֹק, that is, the fixing of set times for Torah, the rains would descend בְּעִתָּם. And what he said — ״וְאֶת מִצְוֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ״ — that is the goal that emerges from the study, for it is what brings about the observance of the commandments, both negative and positive. And regarding negative commandments he said ״וְאֶת מִצְוֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ״, for wherever הִשָּׁמֶר ('take heed') פֶּן ('lest') and אַל ('do not') are stated, it is nothing but a negative commandment (Eruvin 96a). ״וַעֲשִׂיתֶם אֹתָם״ — that is, positive commandments. And since he did not say תִּשְׁמְרוּם וַעֲשִׂיתֶם — why does he need to write ״אֹתָם״? Hear from this that he wished to say that commandments require intention (כַּוָּנָה), for this is what is indicated by the word ״אֹתָם״." "And afterward he specified the reward for each one of these three. Corresponding to Torah study, which is alluded to in ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ״, he said ״וְנָתַתִּי גִּשְׁמֵיכֶם בְּעִתָּם״. He did not say merely 'rains,' because now he did not wish to promise the reward for Torah study — for this matter is already well known, that the rains descend in the merit of Torah, and they are already גִּשְׁמֵיכֶם ('your rains'). Rather, now something additional: that those same rains which are already 'your rains' and designated for you — now I will give them בְּעִתָּם, in the merit of ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ״, and in the manner explained above. ״וְנָתְנָה הָאָרֶץ יְבוּלָהּ״ — for if there is Torah there is grain. And the meaning of ״יְבוּלָהּ״ is not what you bring to it, in the manner that Rashi explained regarding the verse ״וְלֹא תִתֵּן אַרְצְכֶם אֶת יְבוּלָהּ״ — for if so, what is its superfluity? But further on Rashi derived from the superfluity of the word ״אֶת״, for every ״אֶת״ comes to include — and it includes even what you bring to it, you shall not give. But here ״אֶת״ is not stated, and if so, everything is included in יְבוּלָהּ. And he wished to say: what it is its nature to bring to your house — for if not, why is ״אֶת״ not stated here as it is further on? Rather, here the meaning is what it naturally brings, and further on, from the superfluity of ״אֶת״, we derive even what you bring to it. ״וְהִשִּׂיג לָכֶם דַּיִשׁ אֶת בָּצִיר וּבָצִיר יַשִּׂיג אֶת זָרַע״. ״לָכֶם״ ('for you') is not said regarding it, for bread is of the necessities and shall be 'for you,' but wine is of the luxuries; it is not 'for you' for your enjoyment, but rather for the sake of a commandment it shall be. ״וַאֲכַלְתֶּם לַחְמְכֶם לָשֹׂבַע״. The teaching of לָשֹׂבַע ('to satiety') indicates that one should not eat until he is completely satiated, but should eat somewhat less than his fill, in the manner that the physicians have said, so that he comes close to reaching satiety. ״וִישַׁבְתֶּם לָבֶטַח בְּאַרְצְכֶם״. The teaching of ״לָבֶטַח״ ('in safety') indicates that satiety brings about security, as it is written (Psalms 147:14): ״הַשָּׂם גְּבוּלֵךְ שָׁלוֹם חֵלֶב חִטִּים יַשְׂבִּיעֵךְ״, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Metzia 62a): כַּד מִשְׁלַם שַׁעֲרָא מִכַּדָּא נְקִישׁ וְאָתֵי תִּגְרָא — 'when the price of a ka'da-worth is paid, a turner-about comes and it is used.'" "And afterward he promised the reward for ״וְאֶת מִצְוֺתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ״, that is, negative commandments, and said ״וְנָתַתִּי שָׁלוֹם בָּאָרֶץ״, in the manner our Sages of blessed memory said (Vayikra Rabbah 4:4): Guard My lamp in your hand and I will guard your lamp in My hand. And simple שְׁמִירָה ('guarding') means to refrain from doing an evil, harmful thing; and if you are on guard not to do things harmful to you, I too will guard your lamp from every harmful thing, and let there be peace in your camp. ״וְחֶרֶב לֹא תַעֲבֹר״ etc. — because all the observance of negative commandments is through שֵׁב וְאַל תַּעֲשֵׂה ('sit and do not do'), so too the guarding against the sword and evil beast is through שֵׁב וְאַל תַּעֲשֵׂה. Therefore it says: ״וְנָפְלוּ לִפְנֵיכֶם לֶחָרֶב״ — that is to say, even though you pursue them, there is no need for this pursuit in the mode of קוּם וַעֲשֵׂה ('rise and do'), but rather in the mode of שֵׁב וְאַל תַּעֲשֵׂה — on their own they will fall by the sword. And what he said — ״וְרָדְפוּ מִכֶּם חֲמִשָּׁה מֵאָה״ etc. — that the arithmetic does not accord, the commentators said that at first, before your fear enters their hearts, five will pursue a hundred, and after this, when the enemies see that five pursue a hundred, then a great terror will enter the hearts of the enemies and their strength will weaken, until a hundred of you pursue a myriad (רְבָבָה), and after that an even greater terror will be added upon them, and נָפְלוּ אֹיְבֵיכֶם לִפְנֵיכֶם מֵעַצְמָם — your enemies will fall before you on their own, even without any pursuit whatsoever." "And from ״וְנָתַתִּי מִשְׁכָּנִי בְּתוֹכְכֶם״ he began to promise the reward for the fulfillment of positive commandments that are done with the actual hands. Therefore: I will give My מִשְׁכָּן ('dwelling') — that is, the Beit HaMikdash, as Rashi explained — for regarding it is said ״מִקְּדָשׁ יְהוָה כּוֹנְנוּ יָדֶיךָ״, for the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself established it with actual hands, as Rashi explained in Parashat Beshalach (Exodus 15:17). ״וְלֹא תִגְעַל נַפְשִׁי אֶתְכֶם״ — and this is language of rejection (פְּלִיטָה), as Rashi explained. Do not think that when I cause My Shekhinah to rest in the Beit HaMikdash you will be pushed away and ejected (נִפְלָטִים) from there because the bed is too short to stretch out on; nevertheless I will contract My Shekhinah among you, so that we dwell together in one dwelling even though the heavens and the heavens of the heavens cannot contain Him — and you will not be ejected from there. And some say: ״וְנָתַתִּי מִשְׁכָּנִי בְּתוֹכְכֶם״ literally — and do not think that when My soul sees that I have placed the Shekhinah within you, it will reject you and yearn to cleave to the Shekhinah; therefore he said ״וְלֹא תִגְעַל נַפְשִׁי אֶתְכֶם״. And nearby it will be explained in another manner."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:23.237973686Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}